AU  - Lusk, S
TI  - The status of Chondrostoma nasus in waters of the Czech Republic
T2  - FOLIA ZOOLOGICA
CP  - 1st International Workshop on the Biology of the Nase Chondostroma nasus (L)
AB  - Chondrostoma nasus was originally distributed only in the eastern part of the Czech Republic in streams belonging to the Morava (Black Sea) and Odra (Baltic Sea) river systems, and not in the western part in rivers belonging to the Labe system (North Sea). After 1980, systematic stocking with C, nasus resulted in its being introduced into a number of streams belonging to the river Labe system (e.g. Malse, Vltava, Sazava, Luznice, Ploucnice, Ohre, Orlice, and even Labe itself). In some localities natural spawning of the introduced species has already been recorded.
   In the streams of its autochthonous distribution (the drainage areas of the rivers Morava and Odra), C. nasus now occurs from their lower reaches (bream zone) up to the lower parts of the trout zone. In the barbel zone, occupying a substantial part of the major streams, C. nasus is a keystone species. From 1955 to 1965, populations of C, nasus were successively eroded as a result of the increased intensity and accumulation of influences, such as the construction of dams, stream fragmentation, eutrophication and organic pollution. The present status of C. nasus, in its autochthonous range in the Czech Republic, can now be evaluated as ''critical''.
