AU  - Oliveira, MF
AU  - Santos, PO
AU  - de Oliveira, RR
AU  - Figueredo, CC
AU  - Maciel-Silva, AS
TI  - Encapsulating ecosystem engineers: alginate beads of biocrusts for restoration efforts
T2  - RESTORATION ECOLOGY
AB  - IntroductionBiocrusts are pioneer ecosystem engineers that modify the soil surface by aggregating particles, fixing carbon and nitrogen, and retaining moisture. These functions facilitate the establishment of both cryptogams and vascular plants, making biocrusts increasingly important in ecological restoration. Although recent advances have introduced scalable inoculation methods, the use of alginate beads for delivering biocrust organisms remains underexplored and lacks standardized procedures.ObjectivesThis study develops a protocol for producing alginate beads with biocrust organisms and evaluates their short- and long-term viability, focusing on establishment and propagation after storage.MethodsBiocrust organisms-including the mosses along with associated cyanobacteria and algae-were cleaned, fragmented, and encapsulated in alginate beads enriched with starch and Knop I nutrient solution (without calcium). Two distinct organism mixes were prepared based on habitat origin and encapsulated using a standardized protocol with 2% sodium alginate and 1.5% calcium chloride, followed by testing for propagation and viability under controlled growth conditions.ResultsOur results demonstrate that these beads enable the simultaneous inoculation of multiple species, achieving a 100% establishment rate within 21 days. Furthermore, the beads maintain their viability for at least 1 year at 10 degrees C, indicating their potential as a stable and commercially viable tool for biocrust inoculation.ConclusionsThe standardized protocol offers a cost-effective solution for biocrust inoculation for restoration purposes. Next, challenges include assessing the performance of these inocula in the field and their interaction with environmental stressors.
