TI  - THE BEHAVIORAL ECOLOGY OF DINGOES IN NORTH-WESTERN AUSTRALIA .1. THE FORTESCUE RIVER STUDY AREA AND DETAILS OF CAPTURED DINGOES
T2  - WILDLIFE RESEARCH
AB  - This paper introduces a series of papers on a 9-year study of the ecology, social organisation and behaviour of dingoes, Canis familiaris dingo, on the lower Fortescue River in Western Australia. The study area comprised a rugged, undeveloped area and an adjoining area of productive coastal plain developed for grazing sheep. The climate was hot and arid, although rainfall (266 mm per annum) was reliable. In all, 210 dingoes were captured for radio-collaring and tagging. A further 64 dingoes were examined; most had been killed during control work in sheep paddocks. The study population comprised essentially pure dingoes, with little evidence of hybridisation with domestic dogs. Of 256 individuals, 71.6% were ginger-coloured, 15.2% black-and-tan, 6.6% all white, and 6.6% brindle or parti-coloured. Mean (+/-s.e.) weights (males, 18.9+/-0.38 kg; females, 15.2+/-0.63 kg) were equal to the heaviest recorded for wild dingoes.
