AU  - Cavalheiro, MD
AU  - Ferreira, CS
AU  - Miranda, HS
TI  - Single fire events impose lasting reproductive costs in savanna trees
T2  - ANNALS OF BOTANY
AB  - Background and Aims Fire acts as a primary ecological filter, influencing the structure, composition and evolutionary pathways of savanna ecosystems globally. Although numerous savanna tree species exhibit resprouting, the impact of fire on seed quality remains poorly understood. Using Caryocar brasiliense, a keystone tree species in the Cerrado, as a model, we examine whether post-fire trees prioritize vegetative recovery to restore canopy function, potentially limiting resources allocated to reproduction and, consequently, reducing both reproductive output and seed reserve concentrations.Methods Structural responses, reproductive phenology and seed traits were assessed in two 3 ha Cerrado sensu stricto areas: one burned and one unburned. Forty adult trees were monitored for structural responses and 20 individuals for phenological responses over a 30 month period. Seeds from the first post-fire reproductive cycle were collected; 120 were analysed for mass, viability and germination, and 50 were used for quantification of biochemical reserve. We applied generalized linear/generalized linear mixed models and complementary tests.Key Results Although the survival rate was 95 %, the fire caused significant structural damage. Fifty-two per cent of individuals experienced topkill. The number of branches and crown area decreased by 28 % and 56 %, respectively, with minimal recovery observed over 30 months. Reproductive activity was suppressed for two cycles and resumed only after 25-27 months at a reduced level. Seeds from burned trees exhibited lower mass, similar to 20 % lower viability and diminished lipid and carbon reserves. However, germination rates remained similar between treatments.Conclusions Fire-induced structural damage and diminished seed quality demonstrate that a single burn event can restrict the short-term reproductive capacity of Cerrado trees. Such limitations might impede population recovery, highlighting the importance of extended fire-free intervals to facilitate canopy restoration and high-quality seeds. The findings indicate that the structural and physiological impacts of a single fire might endure for considerable periods before the ecosystem restores its pre-fire ecological functions.
