TI  - THE ROLE OF SALIX ACUTIFOLIA AS AN ECOLOGICAL ENGINEER DURING THE PRIMARY FOREST SUCCESSION
T2  - LANDSCAPE AND LANDSCAPE ECOLOGY
CP  - 17th International Symposium on Landscape Ecology - Landscape and Landscape Ecology
AB  - The main aim of this work was to show the role of Salix acutifolia considered as an ecosystem engineer in the processes of succession and soil development under canopy of this species. The results showed that S. acutifolia facilitated the encroachment of early succession species as Corynephorus canescens, Koeleria glauca, Festuca ovina, Jasione montana, Rumex acetosella, Cardaminopsis arenosa, Hieracium pilosella and contributed to formation of primary humus. This is very important stage of ecological succession in ecosystems poor in nutrients. Its role as the ecological engineer concerns in formation of canopy, high production of plant litter and fixing of loose sand. Thereby it creates favourable habitat conditions for encroachment of species with higher nutritional requirements. Single specimens of this species within a field sand form vegetation islands. Connection of several islands will lead to the formation of an initial forest community. Intentionally use of this species for the reclamation of sandy region can bring positive effects. The role of S. acutifolia in soil formation and creating plant communities is connected with its ecological adaptation to the sandy ground. Under the canopy of Salix acutifolia was found the fast soil development processes especially formation of organic (O) and humus horizons in a very short period of time (25 years). The properties of soil under the willow canopy are variable from the trunk to the edge. The high concentrations of organic carbon (OC) and total nitrogen (TN) were observed in the canopy zone.
