A1  - INT EROSION CONTROL ASSOC
A1  - INT EROSION CONTROL ASSOC
TI  - User-rights procedures to promote farmer led natural resource management
T2  - INTERNATIONAL EROSION CONTROL ASSOCIATION - PROCEEDINGS OF CONFERENCE 30
CP  - 30th Conference of the International-Erosion-Control-Association
AB  - The objective of this paper is to share the experience of a pilot project in Amhara Region (Northern Ethiopia) which has attempted to introduce usufruct (or user-rights) procedures as part of a strategy for promoting participatory enclosure and reforestation. The steps of the methodology are described and the outputs and impacts achieved some 18 months after initiating the pilot. The paper also looks at the process by which a small, district-based pilot can influence government policies.
   The experiences to date strongly indicate that the wide-scale introduction of usufruct procedures could have a dramatic effect on community-led natural resource management. Not only does the approach give encouraging ecological results, it also appears to have the potential to be self-replicating at the grass roots level. It may also provide the means for introducing controlled dry season grazing into local farming systems.
   Almost two years after first introducing the idea of user-rights procedures in June 1996, the Amhara State Government has approved the approach and is currently in the process of finalizing new legislation to formalize the approach.
