AU  - Yeates, GW
AU  - Saggar, S
AU  - Daly, BK
TI  - Soil microbial C, N, and P, and microfaunal populations under Pinus radiata and grazed pasture land-use systems
T2  - PEDOBIOLOGIA
AB  - Conversion of improved pastures to Pinus radiata plantations is a significant land-use change and provides an opportunity to evaluate the effects of changes in chemical and biological soil parameters on soil organic matter dynamics. We assessed changes in soil microbial and microfaunal populations for a series of paired sites in the Manawatu region, New Zealand. The change in land-use to P. radiata was associated with declines in soil pH, microbial C and N, soil C and N, soil C : N ratio, microbial C : organic C ratio, total nematodes, most nematode functional groups, and nematode diversity in 0-10 cm mineral soil. These shifts reflect a decline in both the quantity and quality of organic matter input to mineral soil, with lower % organic C contents and wider C : N ratio in soils under P. radiata. While these declines could be regarded as reductions in 'soil quality', such changes involve shifts in soil properties originating from pasture rather than from the original, native vegetation. As the soil microbial biomass and soil microfauna readily recolonize depopulated areas once the trees are harvested, we see few problems in restoring the populations of soil organisms important in controlling nutrient cycling to sites of small woodlots after felling and return to pasture.
