TI  - Soil Environmental Deterioration and Ecological Rehabilitation
T2  - STUDY OF ECOLOGICAL ENGINEERING OF HUMAN SETTLEMENTS
AB  - One important aspect of soil environmental deterioration is land degradation, which refers to the degradation of land quality and productivity driven by unfavorable natural factors and the unreasonable land use of humans. This definition of land degradation suggests that land productivity has significantly reduced and that such decline is driven by human activities or unfavorable natural events. Land degradation processes include the wind and water erosion caused by human activities and habitats, the deterioration of the physical, chemical, biological, and economic characteristics of the soil, and the long-term loss of natural vegetation. This phenomenon is mainly manifested in the reduced biological production of land systems, reduced land production potential, loss of land resources, and poor production conditions on the land surface. From an ecological perspective, land degradation refers to the deterioration of plant growth conditions and decline in land productivity, while from the perspective of system theory, land degradation results from the interaction between human and natural factors. Ecological rehabilitation refers to the process of restoring a damaged ecosystem back to or near its original state and to the related original physical and chemical biological characteristics. This research mainly focuses on two topics, namely, the ecological processes of ecosystem degradation and restoration (including their driving forces and characteristics) and the extant ecological engineering technologies. This chapter also presents a reconstruction model that restores a damaged ecosystem back to or near its original state and reconstructs the physical, chemical, and biological aspects of its structure and function. In view of the different causes, processes, and consequences of land degradation, this work also examines several measures and means for performing ecological restoration.
