TY  - JOUR
AU  - Das, A
AU  - Mishra, S
AU  - Ayejoto, DA
TI  - Harnessing Biotechnology for Sustainable Wastewater Treatment and Circular Economy
T2  - EARTH SYSTEMS AND ENVIRONMENT
AB  - Waste, irrespective of its origin, is one of the most urgent global and human-induced concerns of our day. This results in changes to climate patterns, degradation of ecosystems, and health-related concerns, necessitating both remediation and resource recovery, in accordance with circular economy objectives. Biotechnological solutions that use biological systems, organisms, or their derivatives convert waste into valuable goods, therefore optimising processes that promote sustainable resource management, environmental conservation, and ecosystem restoration. This technology addresses the limitations of traditional treatment procedures by enhancing pollutant removal efficiency, optimising resource recovery, and reducing environmental impact. However, the obstacles involved with the technology include scalability, economic considerations, microbial activity under varying environments, and regulatory demands. Nonetheless, the integration of digital technology and innovations in genetic engineering will be crucial for the enhancement of these systems. Collaboration across governmental, corporate, and civic sectors is essential for a sustainable circular economy and effective waste management. The review discusses the many environmental waste sources in waste water, rational waste management, circularity, sustainable growth, and biotechnological wastewater treatment systems that implement sustainability and circular economy. The study demonstrates that in order to use biotechnology for sustainable wastewater treatment and the circular economy, robust legal frameworks, legislation, and research are required. Biotechnological techniques will be essential to handle increasingly strict environmental rules, circular economy thinking, and climatic change consequences as clean and sustainable water becomes a worldwide issue. Research and development, robust legal frameworks, and regulations will be crucial to the transition and popularisation of biotechnology-driven wastewater treatment.
